  "texts": {
    "preamble": "The State Of West Bengal vs Narendra Nath Kundu on 10 July, 2018 Author: Shivakant Prasad",
    "facts": "The prosecution examined Jahnabi Jiban Laha, Food Inspector, Bolpur Municipality, P.W.1. Dr. T.K. Kar, Assistant C.M.O.H. of Bolpur Local Health Authority and after close of the evidence the accused respondent herein was examined under section 313 Cr.P.C. in respect of incriminating evidence against him that on 17.8.1993 at around 9-00 a.m. in his grocery shop he was found selling turmeric powder.",
    "arguments": "In so far as the impugned judgment and order of conviction relates, there is no denial as such and since no appeal ever filed on behalf of the respondent accused to challenge the order and judgment of conviction, I do not find any illegality in recording finding of facts and finding of guilt of the accused having committed the offence under the provision of the said Act. Mr. Iqbal Kabir, learned counsel for the State/appellant has invited my attention to the order of sentence pointing out that the State has preferred this appeal simply on the ground of legality as to why the respondent accused was dealt with so leniently with the imposition of fine of Rs. 900/- only with the default clause for simple imprisonment whereas the provision prescribes that the fine should not be less than Rs. 2000/-. The penalty as provided in Section 16 (1)(A)(i) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act is imprisonment for a term which shall not be less than one year and which may extend to six years with fine which shall not be less than Rs. 2000/-.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "In so far as the impugned judgment and order of conviction relates, there is no denial as such and since no appeal ever filed on behalf of the respondent accused to challenge the order and judgment of conviction, I do not find any illegality in recording finding of facts and finding of guilt of the accused having committed the offence under the provision of the said Act.",
    "respondent_arguments": "Mr. Iqbal Kabir, learned counsel for the State/appellant has invited my attention to the order of sentence pointing out that the State has preferred this appeal simply on the ground of legality as to why the respondent accused was dealt with so leniently with the imposition of fine of Rs. 900/- only with the default clause for simple imprisonment whereas the provision prescribes that the fine should not be less than Rs. 2000/-.",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "The penalty as provided in Section 16 (1)(A)(i) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act is imprisonment for a term which shall not be less than one year and which may extend to six years with fine which shall not be less than Rs. 2000/-."
  },
